---
import Feature from "./Feature.astro";
import report from "../samples/report.ansi?raw";
import skills from "../samples/skills.ansi?raw";
import failures from "../samples/failures.ansi?raw";
import sources from "../samples/sources.ansi?raw";
---

<section class="features" id="features">
  <p class="eyebrow">Fig. 3 — What it shows</p>
  <h2>Four questions, answered from history you already have.</h2>

  <Feature
    index={1}
    tone="system"
    title="What each session starts with, and what it costs"
    command="quench report"
    output={report}
    truncated
  >
    Per harness: the starting load, peak context, compactions, tool errors, and which models and
    effort levels you actually use.
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    index={2}
    tone="tools"
    title="What loads, and what goes unused"
    command="quench profile skills"
    output={skills}
    wide
  >
    Skills, MCP servers and instruction files, per agent and per project. Quench compares what
    sessions were offered with what they called, so dead weight stands out.
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    index={3}
    tone="mcp"
    title="Failures that repeat"
    command="quench profile failures"
    output={failures}
    wide
  >
    Missing files, missing commands, hook rejections, bad tool input. Grouped by what failed, with
    streaks and how much output each one fed back into context.
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    index={4}
    tone="instructions"
    title="What an agent read, and where its context came from"
    command="quench sources"
    shown="quench sources ~/.claude/projects/…/orders.jsonl"
    output={sources}
    truncated
    wide
  >
    One session, split by source: native file reads, shell reads and searches, MCP calls, skill
    loads and the instruction files the harness injected. Uncertain evidence is labelled, not
    guessed.
  </Feature>
</section>
